# Reference cron.d entry for the Google Calendar pull-sync
# (bin/sync_google_calendar.php) -- see README.md, "Google Calendar
# sync", for the full design.
#
# Install:
#   sudo cp deploy/zpms-calendar-sync.cron /etc/cron.d/zpms-calendar-sync
#   sudo chmod 644 /etc/cron.d/zpms-calendar-sync
#
# Adjust the path below to match your actual deployment root and the user
# it runs as -- needs read/write access to config/db.php and
# config/google_calendar.php/google_calendar_key.json, same as the web
# app's own PHP process. Running it as your existing Apache/PHP user is
# the simplest choice for a single-admin deployment; no separate identity
# is warranted here the way deploy/zpms-backup.cron's own comment
# recommends for backups (this script only ever writes to this app's own
# database, never touches an off-site credential).
#
# Every 5 minutes is the tradeoff this app's own README documents (near-
# real-time would need Google push notifications and a public webhook
# endpoint instead) -- adjust to taste, this script is safe to run more
# or less often.
#
# flock -n guards against two runs overlapping if one run is unusually
# slow (a large backlog of changed events on the very first sync).
*/5 * * * *  www-data  flock -n /tmp/zpms-calendar-sync.lock php /var/www/shtml/apps/zpms/bin/sync_google_calendar.php >> /var/www/shtml/apps/zpms/var/log/calendar-sync.log 2>&1
